   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the village law and the general municipal law, in relation to\nestablishing the village incorporation commission",
    "basePrintNo": "S7537",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7537-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§2-200, 2-202, 2-204, 2-206, 2-208, 2-210 & 2-236, add §2-259, Vil L; amd §239-bb, Gen Muni L",
    "lawSection": "Village Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S7537\n\nSPONSOR: STEWART-COUSINS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the village law and the general municipal law, in\nrelation to establishing the village incorporation commission\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nCreates the Village Incorporation Commission and makes changes to the\nrules for incorporating a new village.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 makes conforming changes to require those filing village\nincorporation petitions to submit filings and deposits with the Village\nIncorporation Commission instead of town supervisors.\n\nSection 2 makes conforming changes with regard to the hearing require-\nments attached to a village incorporation petition, to reference the new\nCommission's involvement in the hearing process.\n\nSection 3 makes changes to the petition requirements attached to a\nvillage incorporation petition, and adds new grounds upon which resi-\ndents can object to a village incorporation petition. These new grounds\ninclude a requirement that a study be conducted on the fiscal, service,\nand taxation interests of the population of the village and the town. An\nadditional new requirement is that the incorporation of the village must\nbe in the fiscal, service, and taxation interest of the populations of\nthe new village and the surrounding town (although village incorporation\npetitions that exist before January 1, 2024 are exempt from this last\nrequirement).\n\nSection 4 changes the entity which makes the decision upon whether a\npetition is sufficient to move forward from town supervisors to the\nCommission.\n\nSection 5 makes conforming changes to reference the Commission.\n\nSection 6 makes conforming changes to reference the Commission.\n\nSection 7 establishes the Village Incorporation Commission. The Commis-\nsion will consist of three members- one appointed by the Secretary of\nState, one appointed by the Comptroller, and one appointed by the Attor-\nney General. The members will serve 4-year terms. Members will be eligi-\nble for reappointment and will serve until their successors are\nappointed and qualified. Terms of successors of any incumbent are calcu-\nlated from the expiration of the term of the incumbent. Vacancies are\nfilled in the same manner as the original appointments. A majority will\nconstitute quorum, and the Commission will decide by majority vote if an\nincorporation may move forward based on the factors outlined in the\nstatute.\n\nTwo studies must be completed for village incorporations- (a) a feasi-\nbility assessment to examine the proposed village's ability to provide\nservices to its population in an efficient manner, including the\nvillage's fiscal capacity to do so, and (b) an impact assessment that\nevaluates the fiscal and operational effects of incorporation on the\nsurrounding town and the rest of the local area. This study will consid-\ner the fiscal, service, political, environmental, and land use impacts.\nThe two studies will be paid for by monies appropriated to the County-\nWide Shared Services Initiative (CWSSI) fund established in Section\n239-bb of the General Municipal Law.\n\nCommission decisions will be posted on the public-facing area of the\nDepartment of State (DOS) website. A moratorium on village elections\nwill be in place until January 1, 2024.\n\nThe Commission must also issue a publicly available report and send it\nto Legislative Leaders and the Governor, regarding an analysis of the\nVillage Incorporation Law. The analysis will look at a number of factors\nconsidered in the process and provide the Commission's recommendations\nand analysis on the adequacy of the village incorporation process,\nincluding but not limited to, whether the requirements of the article\nare adequate to protect the fiscal, service, and taxation interests of\nthe residents of a proposed village and residents of the surrounding\nareas.\n\nSection 8 amends the CWSSI program to create a fund for the purposes of\nstoring monies utilized for Commission purposes. The funds will come\nfrom the CWSSI program.\n\nSection 9 requires that proposed villages contain at least 2,000 regular\ninhabitants, as opposed to 500 in the existing statute.\n\nSection 10 provides for an effective date of January 1, 2024. It also\nprovides that nothing in the Act will deem existing village incorpo-\nration petitions or their signatures invalid. The act applies to all\nexisting and future village incorporation petitions, but existing\nvillage incorporation attempts will continue to be subject to the super-\nvisor approval process instead of the Commission approval process.\nFinally, while all other requirements will attach to existing village\nincorporation petitions, the requirement that the new village be in the\nfiscal, service, and taxation interests of the new village's residents\nas well as the town's residents will not attach to village incorporation\npetitions that exist prior to January 1, 2024.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nA recent analysis by the Elizabeth Haub School of Law at Pace University\nidentified ways in which New York's Village Incorporation Law could be\nstrengthened through legislative reforms. Among these findings were that\nvillage incorporation decision-making should be based upon proper data\nand analysis, that New York's minimum population requirement is inade-\nquate to ensure village viability, and that New York's petition signa-\nture requirements are insufficient, outdated, and unreflective of the\ninterests of those impacted by the proposed incorporation.\n\nThis legislation responds to those findings and recommendations by\ncreating a Village Incorporation Commission that will replace town\nsupervisors in the village incorporation process. The Village Incorpo-\nration Commission will act as the administrative and decision making\nbody for the submission and analysis of village incorporation petitions.\nThis shift allows a more neutral arbiter to oversee the village incorpo-\nration process, thereby relieving town supervisors of the difficult\nadministrative and decision making burdens that come along with the\nprocess. The Commission will more comprehensively analyze the fiscal,\ntaxation, and service interests of the residents of new villages and\ntheir surrounding towns, as well as providing independent analysis to\nLegislative Leaders and the Governor on the adequacy of the existing\nVillage Incorporation Law and how it might be improved.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the state or localities.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately. Creates certain exemptions for\nvillage incorporation attempts that have already filed petitions.\n",
